name,pos,team,player_id,value
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO GET AUCTION VALUES TO THE TOOL
#
# Copy this file to `config/auction_values.csv` and fill it in. The tool reads that path
# automatically; this .example file is never read.
#
# WHY IT IS NEEDED
#   Sleeper publishes no auction-value field over REST (docs/api-findings.md, Finding 3).
#   Every ADP variant is there; there is no `auction`, `auction_value`, `dollar` or `price`
#   key anywhere in the projections payload. So the league's own keeper rule --
#   floor(0.75 * sleeper_auction_value) -- references a number the API will not give us.
#   Without this file, keeper prices are estimates and every figure derived from them is
#   badged as an estimate.
#
# COLUMNS
#   value      REQUIRED. Dollars. `47`, `$47`, `47.0` and `1,200` all parse.
#   name + pos REQUIRED unless player_id is given. Position is confirmed against Sleeper's
#              map, and that confirmation is not decoration: full names collide across the
#              thousands of defensive and offensive-line players Sleeper also carries, and on
#              the current slate two of this league's own keepers share a name with a
#              defender. Name-only matching attaches the value to the wrong player silently.
#   player_id  OPTIONAL. Wins over name resolution when present. This is the escape hatch
#              for a collision the name and position cannot resolve between them.
#   team       OPTIONAL, ignored. Included because exports usually carry it.
#
# Column names are matched case-insensitively and across the obvious synonyms, so a file
# exported from somewhere else will usually just work: `player`/`name`/`player_name`,
# `pos`/`position`, `value`/`price`/`auction`/`cost`/`$`.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS TO ROWS THAT DO NOT RESOLVE
#   They are reported, never dropped. Run `uv run python -m draft_intel.cli value` and any
#   unresolvable row is printed with its line number and the reason. A row that quietly
#   vanished would understate coverage and omit a player from the keeper-price check.
#
# HOW COMPLETE DOES IT NEED TO BE?
#   As complete as you like. Sources are LAYERED, not winner-take-all: every value in this
#   file is used, and the fallbacks fill only the players it does not mention. Twenty rows
#   covering the twenty keepers give twenty real retention prices, and the rest of the board
#   stays estimated and is labelled as such.
#
#   The 20 keepers matter most -- they are what the 75% rule prices. Anything beyond that
#   sharpens the edge figures, which compare what the room will pay against what the model
#   says a player is worth.
#
# Leave or delete these comment lines as you please. Any row whose first field starts with #
# is skipped, as is any blank row, and neither is reported as a problem. The skip happens
# after the file is parsed as CSV, so a quoted field containing a # or a line break is treated
# as content rather than as a comment.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
